    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="27" relname="circumstance">Given this is the beginning of the 4th week of the course,</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="27" relname="span">a lot of our discussions so far have focused on HCI concepts such as user-centeredness, transparency, etc.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="28" relname="span">In this discussion, other concepts are creeping in that are no less important -- things like flexibility, ease of use, ease of learning, consistency, error recovery, and such.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="29" relname="span">Actually, these terms begin to describe the attributes of usability.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="4" relname="evaluation">It is important that we know how to define usability.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="25" relname="span">As you begin to prepare your first assignment paper, Software Evaluation,</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="37" relname="span">this is a good time to discuss usability and usability attributes in depth.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="34" relname="span">Let's discuss these concepts in view of what makes good user-centered design:</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="35" relname="list">ease of use</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="35" relname="list">ease of learning or learnabililty</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="35" relname="list">efficiency</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="35" relname="list">memorability</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="35" relname="list">error recovery</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="35" relname="list">user satisfaction</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="35" relname="list">flexibility</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="35" relname="list">consistency</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="34" relname="elaboration">And we can also draw out usability issues by focusing on the usability principles discussed in our texts.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="33" relname="span">Again, these attributes and principles are important for us to understand the depth the usability beyond our common sense intuition of what we think usability is.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="20" relname="condition">If we have a handle on these,</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="32" relname="span">then we begin to see how we can "measure" usability.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="22" relname="circumstance">As we did with HCI Concepts and Terms,</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="31" relname="span">let's explore these attributes one by one.</segment>
        <group id="23" type="span"/>
        <group id="24" type="span" parent="23" relname="span"/>
        <group id="25" type="span" parent="7" relname="circumstance"/>
        <group id="26" type="span" parent="6" relname="background"/>
        <group id="27" type="span" parent="26" relname="span"/>
        <group id="28" type="span" parent="2" relname="elaboration"/>
